    "body": "I found that `HashUtils.encode` derives its AES key from the caller-provided salt while generating only a random IV and storing the IV with ciphertext. This breaks the analysis tool’s usage rule because the PBKDF2 salt used for key derivation must come from a randomized source. The same change also resolves this rule issue in `deriveKey`.\n\nI now generate a fresh PBKDF2 salt for every new encryption and store it with the IV and ciphertext in an unambiguous versioned envelope. `decode` selects the derivation from that envelope, rejects marked malformed or unsupported envelopes, and retains legacy decoding for persisted IV-prefixed ciphertext. This is safe because current and legacy formats are distinguished before decoding.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime checker for how apps use the Java crypto and TLS APIs.\n\nThe project builds with this change (`assembleDebug`).\n\nI'm a Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), working on a research project on Android app analysis. Happy to adjust this to the project's conventions.",
